11. Row Level Security (RLS) / Data Isolation Rules

Replit Postgres is a plain PostgreSQL with application-level JWT auth (no Supabase auth.uid()). Implement defense in depth:

Layer 1: Application-level scoping (mandatory)
EVERY query touching fields, scans, scan_images, findings, scan_comparisons, chat_messages, weekly_insights MUST include user_id = $authenticatedUserId in its WHERE clause.
Never trust userId from request bodies, params, or query strings. It comes only from the verified JWT (req.user.id).
A resource owned by another user must return 404 (not 403) to avoid leaking existence.
Layer 2: PostgreSQL Row Level Security (mandatory)

Enable RLS and key it to a per-transaction setting app.current_user_id:

sql
ALTER TABLE fields           ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE scans            ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE scan_images      ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE findings         ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE scan_comparisons ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE chat_messages    ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE weekly_insights  ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

-- Repeat this policy pattern for each table above (replace <table>):
DROP POLICY IF EXISTS owner_isolation ON fields;
CREATE POLICY owner_isolation ON fields
  USING      (user_id = NULLIF(current_setting('app.current_user_id', true), '')::uuid)
  WITH CHECK (user_id = NULLIF(current_setting('app.current_user_id', true), '')::uuid);
-- ...same for scans, scan_images, findings, scan_comparisons, chat_messages, weekly_insights

Generate all seven policies explicitly in schema.sql. If the connected DB role is a superuser or table owner (which bypasses RLS), add ALTER TABLE <table> FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY; for each table.

Layer 3: DB helper that sets the context

Create server/db/withUser.js:

js
// Runs fn(client) inside a transaction with RLS context set for this user.
export async function withUser(pool, userId, fn) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query('BEGIN');
    await client.query("SELECT set_config('app.current_user_id', $1, true)", [userId]);
    const result = await fn(client);
    await client.query('COMMIT');
    return result;
  } catch (err) {
    await client.query('ROLLBACK');
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}

All authenticated data access goes through withUser. The users table is accessed only by auth and profile routes with explicit id = $1 checks (no RLS needed, but never SELECT * to the client; always omit password_hash).

