18. Security Requirements
Secrets: all secrets only in environment variables. .env is git-ignored; ship .env.example only. Never expose GEMINI_API_KEY, JWT_SECRET, or DATABASE_URL to the client (frontend env vars may only be VITE_API_URL).
Passwords: bcrypt cost 12; enforce password policy; never log or return hashes.
JWT: HS256, secret ≥ 32 chars (fail at boot if shorter), expiry 7 days, payload { sub, iat, exp } only. Send as Authorization: Bearer. Store on the client in memory plus localStorage fallback (document the trade-off in README) OR an httpOnly cookie if same-site deployment is used; implement whichever you choose fully and consistently.
Authorization: ownership enforced at the application layer AND PostgreSQL RLS (Section 11). Return 404 for foreign resources.
Input validation: Zod on every body, param and query; reject unknown keys with .strict() on sensitive schemas.
SQL injection: parameterized queries only; never string-concatenate SQL.
File upload hardening: MIME + magic-byte verification, size and count limits, re-encode through sharp (kills polyglot payloads), strip EXIF/GPS, random server-generated IDs (never use client filenames).
Rate limiting: global 300 req / 15 min / IP; auth routes 10 req / 15 min / IP; scan creation 5 / min / user; AI daily quotas per user (Section 13). Return 429 with Retry-After.
Headers & CORS: helmet with a strict CSP appropriate for the SPA and WebGL (worker-src blob:, img-src 'self' data: blob:); CORS allow-list from CLIENT_ORIGIN env only; no wildcard with credentials.
Prompt injection defense: delimiters, untrusted-data instructions, output schema enforcement, and server-side validation of every AI response (Sections 13–16).
Error handling: central handler; generic messages in production; structured server logs with redaction of tokens, API keys and passwords.
Account enumeration: login returns the same message for wrong email or wrong password; registration returns a generic conflict message for duplicate emails only after a constant-time-ish check.
Dependencies: run npm audit, pin versions via lockfile, no unnecessary packages.
Privacy: users can delete their account and all data; no third-party analytics; images never shared; document that images are sent to Google Gemini for analysis in the README privacy note.
HTTPS: enforce trust proxy for Render and set secure cookie flags in production.
Frontend: no dangerouslySetInnerHTML; escape all AI text (render as text); sanitize any markdown if used.
